          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would changing when I eat help?",
          "answer": "The information retrieved did not specifically address the broader topic of intermittent fasting or all general health implications of meal timing. However, it did provide some insights relevant to your heartburn and other conditions.\n\nFor heartburn, changes to meal timing can be helpful. A large meal can put pressure on the valve at the top of the stomach. Eating smaller portions may lead to fewer symptoms than eating the same amount in one sitting. Timing also matters because lying down removes the effect of gravity on stomach contents. Leaving several hours between your last meal and lying down is a common recommendation, especially for people whose symptoms mainly occur at night. (from 'Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers')\n\nFor individuals with type 2 diabetes, meal timing is particularly important if they are taking medications that act at fixed times. (from 'Diet for type 2 diabetes') Also, for those using insulin or similar agents, physical activity can affect blood sugar levels, and precautions like checking glucose and having fast-acting carbohydrates are advised. (from 'Physical activity for type 2 diabetes')\n\nRegarding activity and sleep, some people find that late evening activity keeps them alert at bedtime, while for others it is suitable. It's suggested to test what works best for you rather than assuming. (from 'Physical activity and anxiety: the acute effect, the sustained effect, and avoidance')\n\nThis information is for educational purposes and should not be used for medical decisions. If your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, please consult a medical professional.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
